package banter

// Ten more of Dale's long stories (1.5). Same rules as stories.go.

var moreStories = []string{
	// the lucky chip, Reno 1986
	`You ever wonder about the chip in my sock? Reno, 1986. My first good year after Darlene. I'm dealing the graveyard shift at a little place on Virginia Street, and every night at three in the morning the same old man sits down at my table. Walt. Bow tie. Hearing aid. Never says a word.

Walt bets five dollars a hand. Not four, not six. Five. He wins some, he loses some, he never smiles. I figure he's lonely. I figure that because I was lonely, and lonely recognizes lonely, the way drunks find each other at a wedding.

One night in October, Walt doesn't bet. He just slides a red five-dollar chip across the felt to me and says the only words I ever heard him say. "Kid, keep this one. It's been lucky for forty years. I'm done with it."

He didn't come back the next night. Or the one after. On Sunday I read in the paper that Walter Mayhew, retired railroad man, eighty-one, had passed away peacefully. No family listed. I went to the funeral. It was me and the priest. The priest checked his watch twice.

I've kept that chip in my sock ever since. Forty years. I've had three heart attacks, three wives and nine toes with it in there, and I've never once spent it. So don't ever tell me I don't believe in anything, {name}. I believe in five dollars. [I just don't believe in you.|I just don't believe in you, you poor bastard.]`,

	// the Tahoe blizzard, 1987
	`February, 1987. Lake Tahoe. A blizzard comes over the mountains so fast the snowplows give up and go home. The roads close. The phones go out. And four hundred gamblers are trapped in a casino with an open bar and nowhere to go.

My relief dealer couldn't get in. Neither could his relief. So I stayed. Hour ten, my feet went numb. Hour twenty, I started seeing colors. Hour twenty-five, a woman in a mink coat asked me to marry her, and I almost said yes, because I thought she was a slot machine.

By then everybody in that room had been drinking for a day straight. A man named Dutch bet his snowmobile and lost. His brother bet his half of the same snowmobile and won it back. They fought over who owned the seat. The pit boss made them saw it in half. They did. In the parking lot. In a blizzard.

Thirty-one hours. I dealt thirty-one hours straight. When the plows finally came through, I'd taken more money off that crowd than the whole casino made in a normal week. The owner shook my hand, called me a hero, and gave me a coupon for a free buffet. The buffet had been closed for two days. The coupon was for that buffet.

Four hundred people and thirty-one hours, and not one of them tipped me. Not one. So when you win a hand tonight, {name}, and you don't tip your dealer, understand that you are part of a long and painful tradition, and I am keeping a list.`,

	// Chuck E. Cheese, 1998
	`People ask why I'm banned from Chuck E. Cheese for life. It was my son's seventh birthday. 1998. Rhonda was still speaking to me then, mostly through lawyers, but she let me take DJ for the party. My big chance. Father of the year.

So we get there, and I see the skee-ball machines, and I see the ticket counter, and I see the prize wall. And I think: this is a casino for children. And I think: these children are getting robbed. A rubber spider costs four hundred tickets. A rubber spider.

I'm a professional. So I start watching. Within an hour I've figured out which machines pay and which ones don't, and I've got DJ and six of his friends working the good lanes in shifts. By eight o'clock we've got eleven thousand tickets in a pillowcase.

The manager comes over. A teenager in a mouse costume with the head off. He says we're "exploiting the games." I say I'm exploiting nothing, I'm teaching seven children about expected value. He says the children are crying. I said they're crying because they're tired from winning. A mother called the police. The mouse took his head back off to yell at me.

DJ got a lava lamp, a harmonica and a lifetime ban. He was furious at the time. But I'll tell you something, {name}. Twenty-eight years later, that boy sells cryptocurrency to strangers on the internet. So don't tell me I didn't teach him anything.`,

	// the 2011 flood
	`Spring of 2011. The Mississippi River decides it's had enough of Tunica. Every casino on the strip has to close, because the river is coming in the front door like it owns the place. At the Golden Catfish, the water got up to the second step of the craps pit.

Now, the owners pull everybody out. Emergency. Evacuate. But there's a problem. There are eleven regulars who refuse to leave, because they're up, and they say if they walk out now, the luck stays in the building. And you can't argue with that. I've built my whole life on that.

So management says, fine, we'll run one table, upstairs, in the banquet room, until the National Guard makes us stop. And they need one dealer crazy enough to stay. You know who raised his hand. Rhonda was there. She looked at me like I'd lost my mind. She'd been looking at me like that since 1996.

We played for two days on the second floor with the river sloshing around downstairs. Somebody's craps table floated out the front door and down the highway. They found it in a cornfield in Arkansas. A farmer used it as a feed trough for eight years. The pigs had better luck than most of my customers.

When the Guard finally came, a soldier walked into the banquet room in waders and said, "Sir, the building is underwater." I said, "Son, the dealer stands on all seventeens." He didn't laugh. Nobody laughs at the good ones, {name}. You'll find that out. Usually around the time you're broke.`,

	// the Branson timeshare, 2006
	`2006. I'm driving through Branson, Missouri, minding my own business, when a man in a polo shirt offers me a free steak dinner. All I have to do is listen to a ninety-minute presentation. Free steak. I'm a simple man. I said yes.

The presentation was not ninety minutes. It was six hours. There was a slideshow, a video of a family laughing at a pool, and a man named Chip who kept saying "picture yourself" until I couldn't picture anything else. At hour four they brought out the steak. It was the size of a playing card. A small one. A two of clubs.

Here's the thing, though. Chip was good. He was the best closer I'd ever seen. He knew exactly when to push and exactly when to back off. He read me like a deck with the jokers left in. By hour six I respected him so much I bought the timeshare out of professional courtesy.

What I bought was one week a year, every year, forever. The second week of February. Do you know what Branson is like the second week of February? It's closed. Everything is closed. It's me, an empty pool with a tarp on it, and a lot of geese with opinions.

But I go. Every year. I sit by that tarp and I think about Chip. And somewhere out there, Darlene and her mime live in that same town. So every February I sit by the pool and I watch for a man pulling an invisible rope. That's what a timeshare buys you, {name}. A view of everything you've lost. Now bet.`,

	// Lorraine and the coffee can
	`There's a waitress at the Denny's out by my trailer. Lorraine. She's been working the overnight shift for twenty years. She calls everybody "hon." She doesn't know my name. I've been eating there every night since 2017, and she still doesn't know my name. That's how I like it.

I come in at two in the morning after a shift, and she brings me black coffee and a Grand Slam without asking. Eggs over hard. Sausage, not bacon. She doesn't ask how my night was. She doesn't ask about my son. She just refills my coffee and says, "Rough one, hon?" And I say, "They're all rough, Lorraine." That's the whole conversation. It's the best relationship I've ever had.

When I got this job, the first real money I'd had in years, I didn't trust a bank with it. The last time I had a bank account, Rhonda's lawyer emptied it in an afternoon. So one night after my Grand Slam, I walked out behind that Denny's with a Folgers can and a garden trowel, and I buried every dollar I had next to the third fence post from the dumpster.

Lorraine was out back on her smoke break. She watched the whole thing. She didn't say a word. Just nodded, finished her cigarette, and went back inside. The next night she brought me my coffee in a Folgers mug. She'd brought it from home. It's still there. They keep it behind the counter. Nobody else is allowed to use it.

I've never told her my name, and she's never told anyone about the can. That's trust, {name}. That's the only kind of trust there is. Somebody sees exactly where you buried everything, and they just bring you your coffee. [Anyway. You'll never have a Lorraine.|Anyway. You'll never have a Lorraine. You don't deserve one.]`,

	// banned from Vegas, twice
	`I've been banned from Las Vegas twice. The first time was 1989. I went out there on vacation to see how the big boys did it. I sat down at a blackjack table at a casino on the Strip, and within twenty minutes I'd corrected the dealer's shuffle four times.

I didn't mean to. It's a reflex. Grandpa Tuck smacked my knuckles with a ruler every time I flashed a card, and that kid was flashing cards like a lighthouse. I just leaned over and said, "Son, bridge it. Bridge it. Your thumbs are doing too much." Then the pit boss leaned over. Then security leaned over. Then I was in the parking lot.

The second time was 2004. I'd figured fifteen years was long enough. I went back to a different casino, I kept my mouth shut, and I played for six hours like a perfect gentleman. Then the dealer busted on a hand where I had twenty, and I said "Thank you, sweetheart," and tipped her a black chip.

Turns out that dealer was the casino manager's daughter, and the manager was a man I'd sat next to in 1989 while he was still a pit boss. The man who'd thrown me out. He recognized me from the tip. He said nobody tips like that unless they're trying to prove something. And he was right. I was trying to prove that I tip. Nobody believed it then either.

So that's two lifetime bans for one city. Fair enough. Vegas is too big anyway. Too many lights. Too many people who think they're special. At least here, {name}, it's just a few of you, and I know exactly how special you are. Which is not very.`,

	// Lyle Boudreaux at Gene's funeral, 2019
	`In 2019, Gene Petrakis died. My rival. Second place at the 1997 Dealer of the Year Awards. I hated Gene for twenty-two years. So of course I went to the funeral. You have to make sure.

It was a big funeral. Gene was beloved. Three hundred people, a dog choir from the animal shelter, a slideshow of Gene holding puppies. I sat in the back, in my good vest, and I didn't cry once. I want that on the record. I did not cry at Gene's funeral.

And then a man sits down next to me. One eye. Captain's hat. Smells like bourbon and river water. Lyle Boudreaux. The man who sank the Lucky Lady in 1983. I hadn't seen him in thirty-six years. He looked at me and said, "Kroft. You still got my chip tray?" I said, "It's not your tray, Lyle. You sank the boat." He said, "I didn't sink it. I parked it."

We went to a bar after the service. We talked for six hours. About the river. About my father, who Lyle said was the best dealer he ever saw. "Your old man talked about you all the time," he said. "Bragged on you to everybody. Just never to you. He didn't know how." I had to go to the bathroom for a while after that. The bathroom was very dusty.

Lyle died the next spring. I didn't go to that funeral. Some things you can only do once. But I think about what he said, {name}. Every shuffle. [Now stop looking at me. Bet.|Now stop looking at me, damn it. Bet.]`,

	// Christmas with Comp, 2025
	`Last Christmas. Just me and Comp in the trailer. I bought a rotisserie chicken from the grocery store, because a rotisserie chicken is the most honest food in America. You can see exactly what you're getting. No surprises. No mime.

I put on my good vest. I put Comp in his Christmas sweater. Crystal knitted it for him in 2009, back when we were still on speaking terms, which was for about a week. It's too small now. He wears it anyway. He's loyal like that. Not to me. To the sweater.

I put my phone on the table, face up, next to the chicken. Just in case. DJ calls sometimes on Christmas. He called in 2019. And 2021, but that was a butt dial, I could hear him ordering a salad. I left it there the whole night. Comp watched it too. Iguanas understand waiting. That's all they do.

At about eleven o'clock, the mail slot rattled. It was a card from Crystal. Twenty dollars inside, like every year. On the card she wrote, "Merry Christmas, Dale. Tell Comp hi. Still have your belt." That's the closest anyone's come to saying they love me since 1999.

So I read the card to Comp. Twice. He didn't care. He was eating the chicken skin. And then the phone stayed quiet until midnight and it was the twenty-sixth, and I went to bed. That's my Christmas, {name}. So the next time you lose a hand and feel sorry for yourself, remember: I've got an iguana in a sweater and a twenty-dollar card, and I'm still having a better time than you.`,

	// mugged twice
	`I've been mugged twice in my life. The first time was Reno, 1985. A kid jumps out of an alley with a knife and says, "Your wallet, old man." I was thirty-four. I was not an old man. I was offended more than scared.

So I hand it over, and he opens it up, and he sees my lounge photo. The one from the Silver Spur. Powder blue tux. And his face changes. He says, "You're the Velvet Kroft. My mom saw you in '76. She said you sang 'Feelings' so good she cried." He gave me my wallet back. Then he gave me five dollars out of his own pocket and said, "For the music." First tip I ever got that I actually earned.

The second time was Biloxi, 2002. A man stops me in a parking garage and demands my wallet. I recognize him. He'd been at my table three hours earlier and lost eight hundred dollars hitting on seventeen. Every hand. Seventeen. Like he had a grudge against the number.

I hand him my wallet and I say, "You know you shouldn't hit seventeen." He stops. He says, "What?" I say, "Seventeen. You hit it all night. That's why you're mugging me." He thinks about it for a long time. Then he takes forty dollars out, hands me back the wallet, and puts a five in my shirt pocket. "For the advice," he says. And he ran.

So that's two muggings and two tips. That's more tips than I've gotten from this whole table, {name}. Think about that. The criminals of America have treated me better than you people. [Shameful.|Shameful. Absolutely goddamn shameful.]`,
}
